  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Joel invested $$11\\,280.$ The investment performed poorly, losing one-eighth of its value every month.\n\nThe function $p(t)$ models the value of the investment after $t$ months and is given by\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?",
  "worked_solution": "The initial investment was $$11\\,280$, and this decreased by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month.\n\nIf the investment value is reduced by $\\frac{1}{8},$ then $\\frac{7}{8}$ of its value remained every month. So the decay factor is $\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\np=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=11\\,280$ is the initial investment.\n\n- $b=\\frac{7}{8}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p(t)$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{7}{8}.$",
